WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.433
Input validation checks whether incoming data is acceptable before the application relies on it.

2
00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:17.461
Validation should be tied to the business meaning of the field: type, length, format, range, allowed values, required fields, and relationship to other values.

3
00:00:17.461 --> 00:00:21.137
Validation is not only for forms.

4
00:00:21.137 --> 00:00:32.164
API payloads, file names, uploaded content, headers, cookies, query parameters, webhook bodies, queue messages, and configuration values can all carry input that affects behavior.

5
00:00:32.164 --> 00:00:39.976
Injection risks appear when untrusted input changes the meaning of a command, query, expression, template, or document.

6
00:00:39.976 --> 00:00:48.706
SQL injection is the classic example: input is interpreted as part of a database query instead of as data.

7
00:00:48.706 --> 00:00:55.139
Command injection is similar, but the destination is an operating system command or shell.

8
00:00:55.139 --> 00:01:01.112
Template injection can occur when user-controlled content is interpreted by a template engine.

9
00:01:01.112 --> 00:01:10.762
Strong defenses use safe APIs, parameterized queries, careful command construction, allowlisted options, and avoidance of interpreters when a simpler API exists.

10
00:01:10.762 --> 00:01:16.276
Output encoding prepares data safely for the place it will be rendered.

11
00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:22.708
Cross-site scripting, or XSS, occurs when untrusted content executes in a user browser context.

12
00:01:22.708 --> 00:01:31.898
The right defense depends on context: HTML text, HTML attributes, JavaScript, CSS, URLs, and JSON all have different encoding rules.

13
00:01:31.898 --> 00:01:35.574
Validation and encoding are complementary.

14
00:01:35.574 --> 00:01:44.764
Validation rejects unacceptable data; encoding preserves data while preventing it from being interpreted as code in a specific output context.

15
00:01:44.764 --> 00:01:52.116
Secure web applications do both, and they choose defenses based on where the data goes next.
